From 269feaa4f6b8b3b66c597a0b9e27d938be6cc2f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Caleb Jones <caleb@calebgj.io>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:17:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] arch, hypervisor, vmm: riscv64: Describe guest ISA from
 KVM in the FDT

The riscv64 CPU nodes only carried the deprecated "riscv,isa" string,
built from the host's /proc/cpuinfo. Two problems with that:

- Kernels built with CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_FALLBACK=n (e.g. Ubuntu 26.04)
  no longer parse "riscv,isa" and reject every hart:
  "CPU with hartid=0 is invalid: this kernel does not parse riscv,isa",
  followed by a BUG in of_parse_and_init_cpus().
- The host ISA is not the guest ISA. KVM may not expose every host
  extension to the guest (vendor or newer extensions), and the guest
  would then use instructions or CSRs that trap.

Query the extensions enabled for the vCPU from the KVM ISA_EXT one-reg
interface and emit "riscv,isa-base" and "riscv,isa-extensions", keeping
a matching "riscv,isa" string for older kernels. Fall back to the host
ISA when the hypervisor can't report it.

Signed-off-by: Caleb Jones <caleb@calebgj.io>
